1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:09,100
The Allocation Scandal. 55 million people die annually. The National Institutes of Health has an annual budget of approximately

2
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:13,950
forty-seven billion dollars. You would think those two facts would be related.

3
00:00:14,620 --> 00:00:20,000
They are not. Of two hundred forty-seven billion dollars the N.I.H.

4
00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:27,240
spent over a decade, exactly 3.3 percent went to testing whether treatments actually work in humans.

5
00:00:27,940 --> 00:00:37,300
The other 96.7 percent went to everything else. That might be defensible if we'd already tested all the known safe compounds

6
00:00:37,300 --> 00:00:45,780
that are piled up waiting for human testing. We haven't. There are roughly nine thousand five hundred compounds with established

7
00:00:45,780 --> 00:00:53,380
safety profiles, and ninety-nine point seven percent of their possible disease applications have never been tested.

8
00:00:54,300 --> 00:00:58,880
The pipeline is overflowing with candidates. The bottleneck is trials.

9
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:07,060
It's like spending 97 percent of your grocery budget on cookbooks and 3 percent on food, then wondering why you're starving.

10
00:01:07,860 --> 00:01:12,500
This isn't just inefficiency. It's a body count. A J.A.M.A.

11
00:01:12,620 --> 00:01:17,380
study broke down two hundred forty-seven billion dollars in N.I.H.

12
00:01:17,720 --> 00:01:23,520
spending (2010-2019). Each of these categories funds valuable work.

13
00:01:23,940 --> 00:01:29,640
The question is whether the ratio makes sense when thousands of safe compounds sit untested.

14
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:38,660
Basic research accounts for eighty-four point nine percent, or two hundred nine point nine billion dollars, and focuses

15
00:01:38,660 --> 00:01:43,440
on understanding disease mechanisms, molecular biology, and genomics.

16
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:52,680
It is essential for identifying new targets, but 85 cents of every dollar is a lot of understanding and not much testing.

17
00:01:54,140 --> 00:02:02,520
Applied research (non-trial) accounts for eleven point eight percent, or twenty-nine point three billion dollars, translating

18
00:02:02,520 --> 00:02:08,479
discoveries toward practical use, but without the final step of testing them in patients.

19
00:02:09,180 --> 00:02:17,840
The Clinical and Translational Science Awards, or C.T.S.A. infrastructure program, spends approximately one billion dollars

20
00:02:17,840 --> 00:02:21,700
per year in trial infrastructure, coordination, and training.

21
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:30,240
Observational studies collect population health data, such as the All of Us program which cost two point one six billion

22
00:02:30,240 --> 00:02:37,780
dollars. They are valuable for identifying patterns, but observation without intervention doesn't cure anyone.

23
00:02:38,900 --> 00:02:45,000
Other applied research includes drug characterization, health services research, and epidemiology.

24
00:02:45,880 --> 00:02:52,480
Phased clinical trials receive only three point three percent of the budget, or eight point one billion dollars.

25
00:02:53,220 --> 00:03:00,460
This is the only category that directly tests whether treatments work in humans through Phases One to Three.

26
00:03:01,340 --> 00:03:08,240
Phase One receives one point five billion dollars, representing eighteen point five percent of the clinical trial budget.

27
00:03:09,220 --> 00:03:14,910
Phase Two receives three point five billion dollars, or forty-three point two percent.

28
00:03:15,360 --> 00:03:20,480
Phase Three receives two point six billion dollars, or thirty-two point one percent.

29
00:03:21,620 --> 00:03:29,260
The N.I.H. covers only three point seven to four point three percent of Phase Three costs, leaving completion to industry,

30
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,700
which only funds trials with patent-protected profit potential.

31
00:03:34,180 --> 00:03:41,120
Pragmatic trials are thirty times cheaper but remain severely underfunded despite proven efficiency.

32
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:46,860
Why This Allocation Exists. This isn't bureaucratic accident.

33
00:03:47,260 --> 00:03:56,460
It's lobbied outcome. Drug companies love the N.I.H. Taxpayers absorb the risk of basic research; industry patents whatever

34
00:03:56,460 --> 00:04:02,460
works. It's like if your neighbor paid for your kitchen renovation, and then you charged them rent to eat there.

35
00:04:02,980 --> 00:04:07,600
But independent trials comparing drugs head-to-head? Those are terrifying.

36
00:04:08,420 --> 00:04:13,080
They might reveal that the eighty thousand dollar drug works about as well as the four dollar generic.

37
00:04:14,080 --> 00:04:20,360
PhRMA, the pharmaceutical lobby, has consistently opposed comparative effectiveness research.

38
00:04:20,760 --> 00:04:27,820
When Congress created the Patient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute, or P.C.O.R.I., in twenty-ten, industry successfully

39
00:04:27,880 --> 00:04:32,060
lobbied to prohibit it from making coverage recommendations based on cost-effectiveness.

40
00:04:33,780 --> 00:04:38,740
Congress literally passed a law saying "don't fund research that might lower drug prices."

41
00:04:39,660 --> 00:04:45,700
They wrote it down. In legislation. On purpose. The revolving door helps.

42
00:04:45,760 --> 00:04:48,900
N.I.H. advisory committees include industry representatives.

43
00:04:49,580 --> 00:04:55,060
Former N.I.H. officials join pharmaceutical boards. Academic researchers depend on industry grants.

44
00:04:56,160 --> 00:05:03,360
Everyone's incentives align against cheap, fast trials testing whether expensive drugs outperform generics.

45
00:05:04,200 --> 00:05:12,640
It's not a conspiracy. It's just that everyone involved happens to benefit from the same terrible outcome, purely by coincidence,

46
00:05:12,720 --> 00:05:17,700
repeatedly, for decades. Basic research produces publications.

47
00:05:18,180 --> 00:05:25,180
Publications produce tenure. Tenure produces more grants. Nobody in this cycle needs anyone to get better.

48
00:05:25,700 --> 00:05:31,130
They need papers. The three point three percent allocation isn't a mystery.

49
00:05:31,360 --> 00:05:36,340
It's a business model. The Efficiency Gap: A Tale of Two Trials.

50
00:05:37,380 --> 00:05:42,220
The difference between efficient and inefficient trial design isn't theoretical.

51
00:05:42,840 --> 00:05:47,660
Humanity accidentally ran a controlled experiment on itself during COVID.

52
00:05:48,180 --> 00:05:52,420
Two countries. Same disease. One spent like it was trying to cure death.

53
00:05:53,100 --> 00:05:58,140
The other actually did. RECOVER Initiative (N.I.H. Approach).

54
00:05:58,260 --> 00:06:06,460
Budget: one point six six five billion dollars (one point one five billion plus five hundred fifteen million dollars in

55
00:06:06,460 --> 00:06:11,060
twenty twenty-four) (source). Timeline: four years and counting.

56
00:06:11,760 --> 00:06:16,540
Patients enrolled: approximately thirty thousand. Trials completed: zero.

57
00:06:17,200 --> 00:06:20,700
Cost per patient: fifty-five thousand five hundred dollars.

58
00:06:21,580 --> 00:06:26,580
RECOVERY Trial (U.K. Approach). Budget: twenty million dollars.

59
00:06:27,100 --> 00:06:31,000
Timeline: six months. Patients enrolled: forty-eight thousand.

60
00:06:31,600 --> 00:06:37,040
Treatments found: Multiple, including dexamethasone (saved over one million lives).

61
00:06:37,740 --> 00:06:47,820
Cost per patient: five hundred dollars. The N.I.H. spent ONE HUNDRED THIRTY-THREE TIMES MORE PER PATIENT and achieved INFINITELY

62
00:06:47,820 --> 00:06:55,560
LESS (dividing by zero trials completed produces infinity, which is fitting because that's how long patients waited).

63
00:06:56,440 --> 00:07:05,100
This efficiency gap is not unique to RECOVERY. A systematic review of sixty-four pragmatic trials found a median cost of

64
00:07:05,100 --> 00:07:12,260
ninety-seven dollars per patient. The British cured disease for the price of a nice dinner per patient.

65
00:07:13,060 --> 00:07:17,980
America spent the price of a luxury SUV per patient and cured nothing.

66
00:07:18,880 --> 00:07:23,900
The problem isn't that curing disease is hard. The N.I.H. doesn't have a capability problem.

67
00:07:24,560 --> 00:07:29,900
It has a shopping addiction. The Death Toll: Opportunity Cost of Misallocation.

68
00:07:30,880 --> 00:07:38,860
When you misallocate forty billion dollars a year into low-efficiency research instead of high-efficiency clinical trials,

69
00:07:39,360 --> 00:07:47,780
you aren't just wasting money. You are composting people. Cost Per Quality-Adjusted Life Year, or Q.A.L.Y.

70
00:07:48,400 --> 00:07:53,220
Standard N.I.H. Portfolio: fifty thousand dollars per Q.A.L.Y.

71
00:07:53,220 --> 00:07:59,280
(and that's being generous). Pragmatic Platform Trials: four dollars per Q.A.L.Y.

72
00:07:59,700 --> 00:08:08,500
Every one dollar spent on efficient pragmatic trials buys twelve thousand five hundred times more health than a dollar spent

73
00:08:08,500 --> 00:08:17,760
on the current N.I.H. portfolio. The ADAPTABLE trial proved pragmatic trials cost thirty times less than traditional randomized

74
00:08:17,760 --> 00:08:24,300
controlled trials, or R.C.T.s (fourteen million dollars versus four hundred twenty million dollars).

75
00:08:25,060 --> 00:08:30,700
Same science. Fewer catered meetings. The "Death Equivalent" of Budget Misallocation.

76
00:08:31,580 --> 00:08:39,600
The annual opportunity cost is approximately one hundred million quality-adjusted life years, or Q.A.L.Y.s, and approximately

77
00:08:39,600 --> 00:08:48,540
seven million death-equivalents per year. Even the most conservative estimate suggests approximately ten million quality-adjusted

78
00:08:48,620 --> 00:08:53,900
life years, or Q.A.L.Y.s, and seven hundred thousand preventable deaths annually.

79
00:08:54,980 --> 00:09:00,460
You are operating at approximately two percent of your potential capacity to save lives.

80
00:09:01,160 --> 00:09:08,460
If your car ran at two percent efficiency, you'd walk. If your heart ran at two percent efficiency, you'd die.

81
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:14,380
Your medical research system runs at two percent efficiency, and you gave it a raise.

82
00:09:15,240 --> 00:09:22,490
The Translation Crisis: All Theory, No Medicine. The problem is not a shortage of ideas.

83
00:09:22,760 --> 00:09:30,980
It is a shortage of anyone bothering to test them. More than twenty thousand F.D.A.-approved drugs are known to be safe

84
00:09:30,980 --> 00:09:38,280
in humans. Approximately four thousand seven hundred to six thousand eight hundred clinically-tested repurposable compounds

85
00:09:38,280 --> 00:09:44,180
(including three thousand four hundred twenty-two marketed drugs) are gathering dust.

86
00:09:44,940 --> 00:09:52,560
There are hundreds of plausible dementia interventions. There are zero clinical trials available for your grandma in Saint

87
00:09:52,560 --> 00:09:59,440
Louis. You have a warehouse of safe molecules that have never been systematically tested for new uses.

88
00:10:00,220 --> 00:10:04,820
The warehouse is full. The testing facility is closed for a team-building retreat.

89
00:10:05,920 --> 00:10:14,140
The N.I.H. is, by its own metrics, a publishing house that occasionally dabbles in medicine: Papers published: two point

90
00:10:14,140 --> 00:10:19,980
five million annually. New treatments approved: approximately fifty annually.

91
00:10:20,500 --> 00:10:25,860
The conversion rate from paper to patient is zero point zero zero two percent.

92
00:10:26,460 --> 00:10:34,640
For every fifty thousand papers, one drug reaches a human. The rest become citations in other papers, which become citations

93
00:10:34,640 --> 00:10:39,400
in other papers, in an infinite loop of academics reading each other's homework.

94
00:10:40,300 --> 00:10:47,120
Plenty of Knowledge. No Translation. Approximately five percent of patients have participated in trials.

95
00:10:47,880 --> 00:10:52,200
Approximately fifty percent of patients are willing to participate if invited.

96
00:10:52,940 --> 00:10:57,240
Approximately nine to eleven percent of patients are actually invited.

97
00:10:57,940 --> 00:11:02,880
Half of all patients would join a clinical trial if asked. Almost nobody asks.

98
00:11:03,640 --> 00:11:08,860
It's like having a room full of volunteers and a clipboard, and choosing to study the clipboard.

99
00:11:10,260 --> 00:11:14,020
Understanding disease and curing disease are completely different activities.

100
00:11:14,540 --> 00:11:20,800
One requires microscopes. The other requires giving people medicine and seeing if they stop dying.

101
00:11:21,320 --> 00:11:26,600
The N.I.H. chose microscopes. The Public Goods versus Club Goods Scam.

102
00:11:27,700 --> 00:11:32,240
The N.I.H. claims to produce "public goods" like open science and training.

103
00:11:32,700 --> 00:11:40,320
What it actually produces is a free R. and D. department for pharmaceutical companies, funded by the same people who then

104
00:11:40,320 --> 00:11:48,520
pay full price for the drugs. Taxpayers funded research behind ninety-nine percent of new drugs (three hundred fifty-six

105
00:11:48,520 --> 00:11:53,960
drugs, from two thousand ten to two thousand nineteen). Industry patents the winners.

106
00:11:53,980 --> 00:12:01,020
The public pays again through high drug prices. When research leads to a dead end, the public eats the loss.

107
00:12:01,460 --> 00:12:06,960
You pay for the lottery ticket. They cash the winnings. The only things the N.I.H.

108
00:12:06,960 --> 00:12:15,160
*should* fund are things private companies *cannot* profitably fund: Open pragmatic trial platforms: thirty times cheaper

109
00:12:15,160 --> 00:12:18,900
than traditional trials, yet receive minimal N.I.H. funding.

110
00:12:19,760 --> 00:12:27,560
Re-purposing generic drugs: over four thousand seven hundred clinically-tested compounds where no patent equals no profit

111
00:12:27,560 --> 00:12:37,200
equals no interest. Comparative effectiveness data: Head-to-head trials revealing which drugs actually work best (pharma's

112
00:12:37,200 --> 00:12:41,560
nightmare). These are the ONLY things the N.I.H. should be funding.

113
00:12:42,300 --> 00:12:47,400
Instead, the N.I.H. explicitly avoids them to "not crowd out industry."

114
00:12:48,560 --> 00:12:55,860
Translation: "We won't run the trials that would lower drug prices, because the people who set drug prices asked us nicely."

115
00:12:56,680 --> 00:13:00,160
The Patient Disconnect: Zero Correlation with Health Outcomes.

116
00:13:01,640 --> 00:13:07,680
Correlation between N.I.H. funding priorities and actual disease burden: zero point zero seven.

117
00:13:08,360 --> 00:13:14,780
That is not a typo. On a scale of zero (random) to one (perfect), the N.I.H.

118
00:13:14,980 --> 00:13:20,620
scores "basically a coin flip." A dartboard would allocate research funding more rationally.

119
00:13:21,880 --> 00:13:26,520
What patients want. Medicine that works. Access to experimental therapies.

120
00:13:27,140 --> 00:13:32,060
Trials they can join. A cure before they die. What N.I.H. funds.

121
00:13:32,660 --> 00:13:37,840
Molecular mechanisms. Papers in prestigious journals. Research empires.

122
00:13:38,200 --> 00:13:43,000
Institutional overhead. Patients don't control the money. Committees do.

123
00:13:43,660 --> 00:13:47,680
And committees optimize for the continued existence of committees.

124
00:13:48,320 --> 00:13:52,460
This is true of all committees, everywhere, since the invention of committees.

125
00:13:53,720 --> 00:13:57,420
Nobody has ever convened a committee that recommended fewer committees.

126
00:13:58,340 --> 00:14:05,140
The Track Record. Since nineteen seventy, the N.I.H. has spent over one point one trillion dollars (inflation-adjusted).

127
00:14:05,780 --> 00:14:12,820
Diseases eradicated: Zero. This isn't because eradicating disease is impossible: The W.H.O.

128
00:14:13,180 --> 00:14:21,040
eradicated smallpox for three hundred million dollars. Jonas Salk developed the polio vaccine in a university lab and gave

129
00:14:21,040 --> 00:14:25,500
it away for free (which is why nobody at the N.I.H. talks about him at parties).

130
00:14:26,720 --> 00:14:31,280
Veterinarians have eradicated multiple animal diseases while the N.I.H.

131
00:14:31,280 --> 00:14:37,520
was forming subcommittees about forming subcommittees. Cows have better healthcare outcomes than you do.

132
00:14:38,600 --> 00:14:43,700
What Would Actually Work. The U.K. RECOVERY trial already proved it.

133
00:14:43,920 --> 00:14:47,480
Five hundred dollars per patient instead of forty-one thousand dollars.

134
00:14:47,580 --> 00:14:52,080
One hundred days instead of years. Over one million lives saved.

135
00:14:52,660 --> 00:14:57,400
The blueprint exists. It is not theoretical. It happened. In England.

136
00:14:57,800 --> 00:15:01,260
During a pandemic. While Americans were arguing about horse dewormer.

137
00:15:02,260 --> 00:15:08,320
The recipe: Pay for results: Treatment equals payment. No treatment equals no payment.

138
00:15:09,180 --> 00:15:14,160
Radical, I know. Publish everything: Including failures (especially failures).

139
00:15:14,980 --> 00:15:18,630
Eliminate gatekeepers: Let patients vote with participation.

140
00:15:19,160 --> 00:15:25,700
Automate administration: Smart contracts instead of committees (committees cannot be trusted; see above).
